  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1142 AM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nUpper high over the Lower Mississippi Valley which will be\nshifting west over the coming days and briefly setup a nice\ndeep southeasterly flow pattern. Later today, models all\nindicating a similar rain pattern to the last 2 days where most\nactivity will be in an arc from the bootheel NE towards TCS. There\nwill be some convergence just east of the area along lee trough\nwhich may help a few storms develop out there and outflows help\ntrigger a few storms. Coverage will remain isolated over the\nlowlands with scattered in the Gila. Storms will die off around\nsunset. For Saturday, the deep southeast flow sets up over the\neast and the Sacs will get going by early afternoon and outflows\nwill trigger lowland storms, mainly focused east of the RGV.\nForecast soundings have about 500-1000J/kg CAPE but shear is weak.\nCould get a few pulse near severe storms, but think locally heavy\nrain will be the main threat with slow storm motion and PW's of\n1.1-1.3\". \n\nSunday has a wide range of solutions. All the models are showing a\nvery slow moving easterly wave moving up the RGV and these\nfeatures have a tendency to not be well handled by the models,\nespecially in regards to precipitation. Moisture will be in place\nat least early on, but the GFS and it's ensembles almost all\nsupport drier air quickly moving in ahead of the feature and keeps\nmost of the CWA dry. The SREF, ICON and EC ensembles showing at\nleast scattered PoPs. NBM PoPs I felt were low with barely\nmentionable for most of the lowlands and so put in at least widely\nscattered everywhere with scattered in the higher elevations. This\nfeature will linger into Monday over the west and expect some more\nstorms to develop mainly west of the Divide.  \n\nBy Tuesday, the upper high becomes established across much of the\nsouthern US and centered near NM. Drier air moves into most of the\narea and will keep any thunderstorm activity in the far west/Gila\nregion. With the upper high moving into the region, temperatures\nwill warm back up into the upper 90s to around 100 Tuesday and\ncontinue warming into Wed and Thu. Yesterday, the GFS going into\nlate Wed and Thu was bringing a very strong trough across the\nNorthern Rockies and breaking down the ridge while it's ensemble\nmean supported the ridge remaining in place. EC was similar to the\nensemble means. Now the models are coming to a compromise with a\nquicker, weaker trough moving across the Northern Rockies and\nthrough the plains and the area ends up on the tail end of a\nfrontal boundary. This will help to increase PoPs again areawide.\nTemperatures may be a little cool on the NBM as H85 temps warm\ninto the 33-35C range for the RGV and there could be some highs\napproaching 105 around KELP, but timing of this frontal boundary\ncould impact highs and we can move up as we get closer.",
